filter: 'type:Features'
---

Nx Cloud makes CI faster and cheaper in two ways:

- [Remote caching](/docs/features/ci-features/remote-cache) skips any task that already ran with the same inputs, in CI or on a developer machine. On Nx Cloud, Ultracache records the files each task actually reads, improving both cache correctness and efficiency.
- [Distributed task execution](/docs/features/ci-features/distribute-task-execution) spreads the remaining tasks across Workers, and _dynamic task packing_ fills each agent by the CPU and memory its tasks use, so fewer machine-minutes sit idle.

In most workspaces, distribution uses about **50% fewer machine-minutes** than remote caching alone.
Teams using Nx Cloud report **40% to 90% faster CI**.

[Run your whole pipeline on Nx Cloud](#run-ci-on-nx-cloud), or [keep your CI provider](#use-your-existing-ci-provider) and add Nx Cloud to it.

## What you get with Nx Cloud on top of Nx Core

|                 | Nx Core                                                                                                    | Nx Cloud                                                                                                          |
| --------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Caching         | [Local cache](/docs/features/cache-task-results), per machine                                              | Adds a [remote cache](/docs/features/ci-features/remote-cache) shared across your team and CI                     |
| Task inputs     | Hashes each task from its declared or inferred `inputs`                                                    | Ultracache records what each task reads in CI and hashes from that                                                |
| Task scheduling | Task graph on one machine, [`nx affected`](/docs/features/ci-features/affected) to skip untouched projects | Adds [distribution across agents](/docs/features/ci-features/distribute-task-execution) with dynamic task packing |
| Failing CI      | You read the logs and fix it                                                                               | [Self-healing CI](/docs/features/ci-features/self-healing-ci) proposes a fix on the PR                            |
| Visibility      | Terminal output for the run in front of you                                                                | [Run history, task analytics, and CI observability](/docs/kb/optimize-your-ttg)                                   |

## Connect your workspace to Nx Cloud

Push your workspace to a remote repository, then run:

```shell
npx nx@latest connect
```

Remote caching works as soon as the workspace is connected.

{% call_to_action title="Connect from your browser" url="https://cloud.nx.app/get-started?utm_source=nx-docs&utm_medium=website&utm_campaign=nx-cloud-signup&utm_content=ci-features" icon="nxcloud" description="Sign in to Nx Cloud and connect your repository without the CLI" /%}

## Run CI on Nx Cloud

Nx Cloud CI runs your whole pipeline on Nx Cloud from one `.nx/workflows/ci.yaml` file, so you don't need a separate CI provider.
A push or pull request on GitHub starts the jobs in that file, and the results show up on Nx Cloud and as GitHub checks.
Remote caching, Workers, and self-healing CI work without extra steps.

{% aside type="note" title="Private beta" %}
Nx Cloud CI is in private beta, and the Nx Cloud team turns it on for your workspace.
To join, [contact us](https://nx.dev/contact/engineering).
{% /aside %}

Your repository must be on github.com and connected to Nx Cloud through the GitHub App.
Personal access tokens, GitHub Enterprise, and other providers aren't supported yet.

### Set up a pipeline

Commit this file on a branch and open a pull request into `main`:

```yaml
# .nx/workflows/ci.yaml
# yaml-language-server: $schema=https://cloud.nx.app/nx-cloud/static/schemas/workflow.schema.json
name: CI

on:
  pull-requests:
    event: PR
    branches: [main]
  main-pushes:
    event: PUSH
    branches: [main]

jobs:
  main:
    runs-on: linux-medium-js
    steps:
      - run: npx nx affected -t lint test build
```

`linux-medium-js` is a pre-built [launch template](/docs/kb/launch-templates) that checks out your repository and installs its dependencies.
Before your first step, Nx Cloud sets `NX_BASE` and `NX_HEAD`, so `nx affected` needs no extra setup.

Each job appears on the pull request as a check named `<workflow> / <job>`.
Nx Cloud CI runs alongside any CI you already have, so CI runs twice until you remove the old workflow.
A newer push to a pull request cancels the older run of the same file.

### Distribute tasks with workers

Add `workers` to a job to run its tasks across several machines:

```yaml
# .nx/workflows/ci.yaml
jobs:
  main:
    runs-on: linux-medium-js
    workers: 3 linux-large-js
    steps:
      - run: npx nx affected -t lint test build
```

Workers start with the job, so they install dependencies in parallel with it.
To match the machines to the work in each run, list three sizes.
A count without a template name reuses the setup of the job:

```yaml
# .nx/workflows/ci.yaml
jobs:
  main:
    runs-on: linux-large-js
    workers:
      small: 3
      medium: 6
      large: 8
    steps:
      - run: npx nx affected -t lint test build
```

For setups shared across jobs and for task rules such as `stop-after`, see [`worker-templates`](/docs/reference/nx-cloud/ci-config#worker-templates).

### Secrets and environment variables

Add secrets on the **Environment variables** page of your [workspace settings](https://cloud.nx.app/go/workspace/settings), and read them with `${{ secrets.<NAME> }}`:

```yaml
# .nx/workflows/ci.yaml
env:
  NODE_OPTIONS: --max-old-space-size=8192
  NPM_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.NPM_TOKEN }}
```

`env` at the top of the file or on a job also reaches the workers of that job, and step logs mask secret values.
Terminal output from tasks uploaded to Nx Cloud isn't masked.

### Self-healing CI

When self-healing CI is on for your workspace, Nx Cloud adds the `Fix CI` step to each main job.
Don't add `nx fix-ci` yourself.
To limit which tasks it fixes, use the [`self-healing`](/docs/reference/nx-cloud/ci-config#self-healing) section.

### Coming from GitHub Actions

The file reads like a GitHub Actions workflow, and most step-level syntax carries over.
This workflow:

```yaml
# .github/workflows/ci.yml
name: CI

on:
  push:
    branches: [main]
  pull_request:
    branches: [main]

jobs:
  main:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    env:
      NX_CLOUD_ACCESS_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.NX_CLOUD_ACCESS_TOKEN }}
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v7
        with:
          fetch-depth: 0
      - uses: actions/setup-node@v6
        with:
          node-version: 24
          cache: npm
      - run: npm ci
      - uses: nrwl/nx-set-shas@v5
      - run: npx nx-cloud start-workers
      - run: npx nx affected -t lint test build
      - run: npx nx fix-ci
        if: always()
```

Becomes this file:

```yaml
# .nx/workflows/ci.yaml
name: CI

on:
  main-pushes:
    event: PUSH
    branches: [main]
  pull-requests:
    event: PR
    branches: [main]

jobs:
  main:
    runs-on: linux-medium-js
    workers: 3 linux-large-js
    steps:
      - run: npx nx affected -t lint test build
```

| GitHub Actions                                     | Nx Cloud CI                                          |
| -------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| `on: push` and `on: pull_request`                  | Named triggers with `event: PUSH` or `event: PR`     |
| `runs-on: ubuntu-latest`                           | A [launch template](/docs/kb/launch-templates)       |
| `actions/checkout`, `actions/setup-node`, `npm ci` | `init-steps` of the launch template                  |
| `nrwl/nx-set-shas`                                 | Automatic                                            |
| `start-workers`                                    | `workers` on the job                                 |
| `nx fix-ci`                                        | Automatic                                            |
| `uses: owner/repo@ref`                             | `uses` with a [step template](/docs/kb/custom-steps) |
| `needs`, `if`, `env`, `timeout-minutes`            | Same keys                                            |
| `${{ secrets.X }}`, `${{ needs.<job>.result }}`    | Same expressions                                     |
| `GITHUB_ENV`, `GITHUB_OUTPUT`, `GITHUB_PATH`       | Same files                                           |
| `github.event_name` in a condition                 | `nxcloud.event_name`                                 |

Required status checks need updating, because the check names change to `<workflow> / <job>`.

### Current limits

These aren't supported yet:

- Pull requests from forks.
- Events other than push and pull request, including `schedule`, `workflow_dispatch`, and merge queues.
- `strategy.matrix`, `concurrency`, `services`, and job `outputs`.
- Actions from the GitHub Marketplace and `action.yml` files, including `actions/cache` and `actions/upload-artifact`. `uses` runs Nx Cloud step templates.
- OIDC identity tokens for cloud providers. Use secrets for now.
- macOS. Jobs run on Linux and Windows.

A job runs for at most 60 minutes, and that is also the default for `timeout-minutes`.
You can't turn off GitHub status checks while Nx Cloud CI is on.
Configuration errors in `ci.yaml` show on the GitHub check.

## Use your existing CI provider

To keep your pipeline on GitHub Actions, CircleCI, GitLab, or another provider, add Nx Cloud to it.
The same [CI configuration file](/docs/reference/nx-cloud/ci-config) sets your workers, and `nx-cloud start-workers` starts them from your main job:

```yaml
# .nx/workflows/ci.yaml
worker-templates:
  default: 3 linux-large-js
```

```shell
npx nx-cloud start-workers
```

For the full pipeline on your provider, see [setting up CI](/docs/getting-started/setup-ci).
If your workspace has a `.nx/ci-config.yaml` file, [migrate it to `ci.yaml`](/docs/kb/migrate-from-ci-config).

## Building blocks of fast CI

Fast CI comes from two things:

- **Get more out of your machine minutes.** [Distributed task execution](/docs/features/ci-features/distribute-task-execution) spreads tasks across Workers so work runs in parallel. Dynamic task packing runs tasks together when their combined CPU and memory needs fit on a Worker, so you get more work out of each machine.
- **Only run what you have to.** [`nx affected`](/docs/features/ci-features/affected) selects tasks whose inputs your changes touch. [Remote caching](/docs/features/ci-features/remote-cache) reuses results from tasks that already ran with the same inputs, so they don't run again.

## Why task-based CI costs less

Most CI setups use static partitioning (binning and sharding), assigning work to machines up front, such as one job for tests and another for builds, and each machine runs until its slowest task ends.

![CI tasks divided into fixed bins](../../../../assets/concepts/ci-concepts/binning.svg)

Nx Cloud assigns individual tasks as agents free up, using the task graph and each task's past duration, CPU, and memory use.
Agents stay busy, an agent that fails during setup has its work picked up by the others, and adding agents needs no change to how work is split.

![CI tasks distributed across three agents](../../../../assets/concepts/ci-concepts/3agents.svg)

[Learn how teams use Nx Cloud](https://nx.dev/blog?filterBy=customer+story) to scale their workspaces.

